# Citation metadata for Sprite Design Datum, in Citation File Format 1.2.0.
# https://citation-file-format.github.io/
#
# WHY `type: dataset`
#
#   CFF 1.2.0 permits exactly two values for `type`: `software` and `dataset`. Neither describes a
#   standards document and there is no third option to reach for, so this is the closer of two
#   imperfect fits rather than a claim.
#
#   `software` is the wrong one. This repository deliberately ships no code, no linter, and no
#   schema — see README, "What it deliberately does not do" — because a machine-readable copy of the
#   values would be a second source of truth for them.
#
#   `dataset` is the conventional CFF filing for a non-software research output, and the
#   load-bearing half of this document is measurement: tolerances recovered from named public sprite
#   corpora with a stated, re-runnable instrument, alongside a register of published external values
#   and a register of quantities that have no published source at all. Filed as `dataset` for that
#   reason. It is not a data file, and this comment exists so nobody reads the field as saying it is.
#
# WHY `url` IS THE DOCUMENTATION SITE AND THERE IS NO `repository-code`
#
#   The repository is private. `repository-code` pointing at it would resolve to a 404 for every
#   reader who is not the owner, which is worse than no field: a reader cannot tell a dead link from
#   a wrong one. The documentation site is where the standard is actually readable, so that is what
#   a citation should carry. Add `repository-code` in the same commit that makes the repository
#   public, if that ever happens.
#
# NOTE FOR WHOEVER MAINTAINS THIS
#
#   `version` below is a THIRD copy of the version string. The other two are the front matter of
#   SPRITE-DESIGN-DATUM.md and the top heading of CHANGELOG.md. All three must move together, and
#   `tools/check.mjs` check 4 enforces exactly that — it reads all three and fails on a mismatch,
#   and takes the tag being cut as an optional fourth. Do not add a fourth copy without telling it.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you cite this standard, or conform a project to it, please cite it as below. Citing it and
  linking to it is the intended use and needs no permission.
title: Sprite Design Datum
type: dataset
authors:
  # CFF splits authors into persons (given-names / family-names) and entities (a single `name`). The
  # author publishes under a handle, and LICENSE's scope section already flags the named-holder
  # question as one a lawyer has to settle — so the entity form is used here rather than inventing a
  # split of a handle into a first and last name.
  - name: HetCreep
url: 'https://hetcreep.gitbook.io/hetcreep-docs'
version: 2.0.1
date-released: '2026-08-11'
license: CC-BY-NC-ND-4.0
abstract: >-
  A geometry standard for 2D sprite art, and a map of which parts of that geometry have a published
  external source and which parts have none. Every value is filed under who locks it — a graphics
  API, a device target, the adopting project's owner, an external convention with real precedent,
  the adopting project's own measurement, or nobody — and a value nobody locks is written as
  unlocked rather than promoted to specification to make a table look complete. Where no authority
  exists, tolerances were recovered by measuring public sprite corpora with a stated, re-runnable
  instrument, and the method is published alongside the result so the numbers can be re-derived
  rather than trusted. The document names its own coverage gaps, including the absence of any
  web-platform source in its register of published values.
keywords:
  - sprite
  - 2D art
  - pixel art
  - texture
  - anchor tolerance
  - game development
  - standard
